          "question": "I have had three headaches this month, all on one side, with nausea and the light bothering me.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The symptom check found no emergency pattern in the red-flag table. That is not a clearance. The table covers a fixed list of presentations, so a book a routine appointment and describe the pattern to a clinician. If anything on the list below happens, get emergency help instead.\n\n**Seek emergency care now if you have any of these:**\n- A sudden, severe headache that reaches full strength within about a minute, or the worst headache of your life. (Migraine guidance says this is an emergency presentation covered elsewhere. Source: *Migraine: acute treatment...*, guideline summary.)\n- Sudden weakness or numbness in one arm, one leg, or one side of the body. (Source: *Sudden neurological symptoms that may indicate a stroke*.)\n- A drooping face, slurred speech, trouble finding words or understanding others, sudden confusion, sudden vision loss or blurring, or sudden trouble walking or balance problems. (Same source.)\n- A headache with fever and a stiff neck, or a headache after a head injury. (Migraine guideline summary lists these as emergency presentations.)\n\n**What the pattern involves:** Your symptoms touch two body systems: the stomach (nausea) and the nervous system (light sensitivity). The headache itself is the common thread. The classic migraine description in the reference notes is a one-sided, pulsing headache of moderate to severe strength, often with nausea and sensitivity to light and sound, lasting several hours to three days. Your description fits that pattern in part. The reference also says migraine is often mislabeled, so a clinician needs to confirm it. Several other conditions are linked to these symptoms in the corpus, but I can't tell you which, if any, applies to you.\n\n**Points worth raising with a clinician:**\n- The three episodes this month. Note how long each lasted, how severe it was, and what came before it.\n- Whether the headaches are getting more frequent or changing in character.\n- How often you take pain relievers. Frequent use can keep headaches going. The guidance says to check for this before any treatment decisions.\n- Any blood pressure readings you have, since very high readings with severe headache need urgent attention.\n\n**Limitation:** I could not retrieve a dedicated headache red-flag document, because the search budget ran out. The points above come from the migraine and stroke notes, so a clinician should check the full list.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
